# One person, two spellings. `git shortlog`, `git log --use-mailmap` and the
# GitHub contributor list read this file, so history reads as one author
# without rewriting a single commit.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not change the email addresses recorded in the
# commits. One of them is a machine-local hostname address
# (a machine hostname address) and the other is institutional. Both stay
# visible in `git log --format=%ae` and in the raw objects once this repository
# is public.
#
# DECIDED, on the evidence rather than on preference. Measured against the
# public repository's own history:
#
#   thirupallikrishnan.n@northeastern.edu   appears in 1 public commit author
#   the machine-local hostname address        appears in 0
#
# So the machine-local line was publishing a machine hostname while mapping an
# address that no public commit contains -- disclosure for no benefit. It is
# removed. The institutional line stays, because it maps an address that IS in
# the public commit objects, which is what makes `git shortlog` read as one
# author; deleting the line would not delete the address.
#
# History is NOT rewritten. The address is already in published commit objects,
# so a rewrite would not un-publish it -- and the last force-push here orphaned
# the shared base and closed six Dependabot PRs that could not be reopened.
# That is a real cost against no gain.
Nirmal Kumar Thirupallikrishnan <thirupallikrishnan.n@northeastern.edu>
